# Security headers for the static page.
#
# Cloudflare's asset handler serves public/ from the edge without invoking the
# Worker, so the Worker's own header middleware never sees these requests -
# which is why the policy is repeated here rather than living in one place.
# The two are kept deliberately identical; change both or neither.
#
# The page loads one web font from Google and nothing else, so the policy is
# strict about everything except that - and, more usefully, about where
# anything may be *sent*: connect-src 'self' means injected script could not
# exfiltrate a reading even if it ran.
#
# That font is the one third-party request the dashboard makes, and it hands
# Google the viewer's IP on every page view. Self-hosting it (Manrope is
# OFL-licensed) or falling back to the system stack would remove that; both
# change how the page looks, so it is left as a decision rather than taken.
#
# 'unsafe-inline' is unavoidable while the script and styles live in the HTML.
# That is the deliberate trade for having no build step.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY

# Chrome rejects a manifest that does not arrive as JSON, and the asset handler
# has no mapping for .webmanifest of its own.
/manifest.webmanifest
  Content-Type: application/manifest+json

# The worker must be allowed to control the whole origin, not just /.
/sw.js
  Service-Worker-Allowed: /
  Cache-Control: no-cache
